findings.body:
The `!` on `hasScheduled.processOutbox` was dropped when the labs check was replaced:

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/member-welcome-emails/jobs/index.js" lines="10-26"}

`hasScheduled.processOutbox` starts as `false` and is only set to `true` inside this branch. The branch is therefore unreachable, and `jobsService.addJob` never runs in any environment. Outbox rows will keep piling up as `pending` and no welcome email is ever sent, so the stress test this PR exists for won't happen. The function also always returns `false`.

::suggestion{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/member-welcome-emails/jobs/index.js" lines="15-15"}
        if (!hasScheduled.processOutbox && !process.env.NODE_ENV.startsWith('test')) {

No test covers `scheduleMemberWelcomeEmailJob`. A small unit test that stubs `config.get` and `jobsService.addJob` and sets a non-test `NODE_ENV` would have caught this.

The gate used to require the flag **and** an allowed source. It now accepts either one:

::diff{file="ghost/core/core/server/services/members/members-api/repositories/MemberRepository.js" lines="340-342"}

This causes two separate regressions:

- **No config set (self-hosted default, `defaults.json` has `""`):** every member whose source is `member`, i.e. all self-signups, still goes through the transactional path and gets a `MemberCreatedEvent` outbox row. That's the "unnecessary impact to self-hosted instances" the PR description promises to avoid. The rows are never consumed and grow without bound.
- **Config set:** imports, admin-created members and API-created members all get outbox rows. They will be picked up once the job runs. A bulk import could then send thousands of welcome emails to the test inbox and skew the stress test.

The PR's own tests catch both cases. `does NOT create outbox entry when config is not set` (unit and integration) and `does not create outbox entry for disallowed sources` / `...when member is imported` / `...created by admin` should all fail with this condition.

